# windows-conemu -- the terminfo entry this renderer's contract is written against.
#
# GENERATED-DERIVED copy of org/jline/utils/windows-conemu.caps as shipped by jline, which is the file that
# is actually READ at run time (terminal/src/main/resources in the source tree, and inside lib/JLine3.jar in
# the install). This one exists so that `tic`/`infocmp` -- tools that will not parse the jline spelling, whose
# first line carries prose with commas in it -- can be run against the same capability set. terminfo_check.sh
# proves the two sets are identical and refuses the CHECK if they are not (it is a hand-run gate, in the same
# list as build.sh and run.ps1; nothing invokes it automatically, so a change to the entry that skips it shows
# up as a stale .ti rather than as a red build); it also compares the on-disk caps with the copy inside
# lib/JLine3.jar and with jline's source copy, because "the entry we audited" and "the entry that ships" are
# two facts. Only the capability FIELDS below are load-bearing: line 0 of the .caps is never parsed by anyone,
# which is why the entry's own long rationale lives there and why editing that prose still means moving all
# three shipped copies.
#
# The rules that decide what is IN here live in ../TERMINFO.md (and the renderer's own dispositions in
# ../ANSI_SUPPORTS.md); the short version of why the omissions are as deliberate as the additions:
#
#   hts, tbc               -- **the boundary here is which parser receives this name, not whether a reader
#                             exists** (#113 rewrote it on 2026-09-28; the sentence this slot used to lead
#                             with -- "no code in this jline reads either one" -- is retired by the owner's
#                             ruling of 2026-09-28 and was never the reason that could stand). Measured that
#                             day in a real ConEmu window with cache/p113/tabprobe.py: CEAnsi does NOT honour
#                             ESC H (a stop set at column 5 loses to the default interval; tab lands on 9) and
#                             its CSI Z / CSI I do not walk a stop table at all, while the same probe against
#                             a classic conhost with VTP on shows ESC H, CSI 0g, CSI 3g, CSI Z and CSI I all
#                             behaving. The entry is named after the parser that drops them, so writing them
#                             would promise a tab stop that evaporates (DESIGN I26). That CEAnsi files ESC H as
#                             de_Ignored (Ansi.cpp:2732) and has no CSI g arm is the source-side statement of
#                             the same fact; the probe is what lets this file say it without a citation nobody
#                             can re-run. This library models all five and proves it: esc_dispatch sets a stop
#                             on ESC H, tab_prev walks the table back, CSI 3g clears it and CSI 5g restores the
#                             interval -- witnessed by geo_tabs, which is #113's acceptance for "implemented and
#                             somebody can see it". Saying so truthfully needs its OWN entry: #117.
#                             cbt DID go in when #70 landed the table, and it is the exception rather than the
#                             same case three times: a reader binds it (LineReaderImpl.java:6420 uses the value
#                             as the name of the Shift-Tab key, and an absent capability unbinds that key in
#                             silence), and nothing in this stack ever writes it, so it makes no promise to
#                             CEAnsi. it#8 is the DEFAULT interval now, overridable by CSI 3g / CSI 5g, not the
#                             whole tab story.
#   smir, rmir, mir        -- IRM is modelled here since #77 (I40: one bit, both spellings, reportable, and
#                             both resets clear it), so this is no longer a "no body" case: the pair stays out
#                             because the entry also describes sessions ConEmu's parser reads, and there
#                             `CSI 4 h/l` is filed as de_Ignored (Ansi.cpp:3323-3329) -- a claim about the
#                             OTHER reader, which I26 forbids. Same shape as smam/rmam below.
#   smam, rmam             -- DECAWM is modelled (#61) but the entry also describes sessions ConEmu's own
#                             parser reads, where its SetConsoleMode is commented out (Ansi.cpp:3268-3281);
#                             declaring the pair would be a claim about the OTHER reader (DESIGN I26).
#   smkx, rmkx             -- DECCKM is an input-side mode and this terminal's input is console records.
#   kmous                  -- DECLARED \E[M since gap A1 landed the encoder seam (2026-10-04): the layer
#                             that reads the console records (dbcli's WinSysTerminal) now hands each MOUSE_EVENT
#                             to this library's rc_mouse_report through JNI and injects the bytes it returns,
#                             so the sender of the reports is this stack and the old "somebody else's business"
#                             ruling no longer holds. The declared string is the legacy prefix the default
#                             family (X10, and 1000/1002/1003 without a format extension) puts on the wire,
#                             asserted against the live bytes per family by cache/gaps/MouseLeg; an SGR
#                             negotiation (?1006+) does not consult kmous for its shape, the same convention
#                             xterm's own entry keeps.
#   blink, invis, ncv      -- SGR 5/6/8 hold no state on either leg, and #94 closed the question at the carrier:
#                             what this library writes is a CHAR_INFO attribute word, and that word has no blink
#                             bit to hold one in. ncv would claim attributes cannot coexist with colour; the
#                             console's answer is carried by the fold in rc_attr() instead.
#   rmpch, sgr10/sgr11 arms -- no alternate-font path (rmacs is ESC ( B, not ESC [ 10m).
#   mc5i                   -- no printer.
#   flash                  -- ESC g flashes the ConEmu window (Ansi.cpp:2728 calls GuiFlashWindow); a renderer
#                             must not, and leaving it out makes readers fall back to bel.
#   initc, ccc             -- OSC 4/10/11 now change the palette (#55), but the entry also describes a session
#                             parsed by ConEmu, which consumes them and changes nothing (I26 again).
#   xenl                   -- measured absent on both writer legs; it is the whole reason `am` is declared.
#   kf13 and up, kLFT/kRIT/kUP/kDN/kHOM/kEND/kPRV/kNXT/kIC/kDC
#                          -- jline's key leg synthesises the bytes for a modified key itself and never asks
#                             caps; terminfo can name only the Shift arm, no widget binds a shifted key.
#   u8, u9                 -- CSI c and CSI >c are BOTH answered, but no code in jline reads user8/user9
#                             (CursorSupport reads user6 and user7 only), so a value here would be invented; and
#                             ncurses spells u8 as a pattern whose %[ our own Curses.tputs refuses to compile.
#
# In edits: kbs=^H stays because jline decodes it through Curses.tputs to 0x08 and binds 0x08 and 0x7f to the
# same widget, and all three Windows entries in this jline spell it that way. rep is in because CSI b has a
# body in the parser and a model in the renderer; its parameter must reach tputs as an INT, because
# toInteger() parses a Character as a String and throws. u6/u7 are in because the answer leg writes the reply
# back onto CONIN$ as key events in the shape CursorSupport compiles u6 into a regex for.
#
windows-conemu|ConEmu ANSI with the native renderer (no xenl: margin wrap is immediate; bce: every erase fills with the live attribute),
	am, bce, msgr,
	colors#256, cols#80, it#8, lines#24, pairs#64,
	bel=^G, bold=\E[1m, cbt=\E[Z, clear=\E[H\E[2J,
	cr=^M, cub=\E[%p1%dD, cub1=\E[D, cud=\E[%p1%dB, cud1=\E[B,
	cuf=\E[%p1%dC, cuf1=\E[C, cup=\E[%i%p1%d;%p2%dH,
	cuu=\E[%p1%dA, cuu1=\E[A,
	civis=\E[?25l, cnorm=\E[?25h, cvvis=\E[?25h,
	smcup=\E[?1049h, rmcup=\E[?1049l,
	rc=\E8, sc=\E7,
	il=\E[%p1%dL, il1=\E[L,
	dl=\E[%p1%dM, dl1=\E[M,
	ich=\E[%p1%d@, ich1=\E[@, dch=\E[%p1%dP, dch1=\E[P, ech=\E[%p1%dX, rep=%p1%c\E[%p2%{1}%-%db,
	el=\E[K, ed=\E[J, el1=\E[1K,
	home=\E[H, hpa=\E[%i%p1%dG, vpa=\E[%i%p1%dd,
	ind=^J, indn=\E[%p1%dS, rin=\E[%p1%dT, ri=\EM, nel=\EE, ht=^I,
	csr=\E[%i%p1%d;%p2%dr,
	kbs=^H, kcbt=\E[Z,
	kcub1=\EOD, kcud1=\EOB, kcuf1=\EOC, kcuu1=\EOA,
	khome=\E[H,
	op=\E[39;49m,
	rev=\E[7m,
	smacs=\E(0, rmacs=\E(B, acsc=``aaffggiijjkkllmmnnooppqqrrssttuuvvwwxxyyzz{{||}}~~,
	rmso=\E[27m, rmul=\E[24m, smso=\E[7m, smul=\E[4m,
	setab=\E[48;5;%p1%dm, setaf=\E[38;5;%p1%dm,
	sgr=\E[0%?%p6%t;1%;%?%p2%t;4%;%?%p1%p3%|%t;7%;m,
	sgr0=\E(B\E[0m,
	u6=\E[%i%d;%dR, u7=\E[6n,
	kdch1=\E[3~, kich1=\E[2~, kend=\E[4~, kmous=\E[M, knp=\E[6~, kpp=\E[5~,
	kf1=\EOP, kf2=\EOQ, kf3=\EOR, kf4=\EOS, kf5=\E[15~, kf6=\E[17~,
	kf7=\E[18~, kf8=\E[19~, kf9=\E[20~, kf10=\E[21~, kf11=\E[23~, kf12=\E[24~,
